    "engine": "jupyter",
    "markdown": "---\ngd-head-tags:\n  - >-\n    `<script type=\"application/ld+json\">[{\"@context\": \"https://schema.org\",\n    \"@type\": \"SoftwareSourceCode\", \"name\": \"EdgeCase\", \"description\":\n    \"High-performance graph analysis library\", \"codeRepository\":\n    \"https://github.com/rich-iannone/edgecase\", \"license\":\n    \"https://opensource.org/licenses/MIT\", \"url\":\n    \"https://rich-iannone.github.io/edgecase\", \"documentation\":\n    \"https://rich-iannone.github.io/edgecase\", \"programmingLanguage\": {\"@type\":\n    \"ComputerLanguage\", \"name\": \"Python\"}}, {\"@context\": \"https://schema.org\",\n    \"@type\": \"WebPage\", \"name\": \"Graph.symmetric_difference - EdgeCase\",\n    \"isPartOf\": {\"@type\": \"WebSite\", \"name\": \"EdgeCase\"}, \"url\":\n    \"https://rich-iannone.github.io/edgecase/reference/Graph.symmetric_difference.html\"}]</script>`{=html}\nopen-graph:\n  title: >-\n    [Graph.symmetric_difference()]{.doc-object-name .doc-function .doc-label\n    .doc-label-method} | EdgeCase\n  description: Compute the symmetric difference of this graph and another graph.\ntwitter-card:\n  title: >-\n    [Graph.symmetric_difference()]{.doc-object-name .doc-function .doc-label\n    .doc-label-method} | EdgeCase\n  description: Compute the symmetric difference of this graph and another graph.\ntitle: >-\n  [Graph.symmetric_difference()]{.doc-object-name .doc-function .doc-label\n  .doc-label-method}\nbody-classes: doc-api-page doc-py-api-page\nshift-heading-level-by: 0\nbread-crumbs: false\ntoc-depth: 3\ndescription-meta: >-\n  Compute the symmetric difference of this graph and another graph.\npage-navigation: false\nhtml-table-processing: none\n---\n\n::: {.doc-subject}\n\nCompute the symmetric difference of this graph and another graph.\n:::\n\n::: {.doc-usage-source}\nUsage\n\n[Source](https://github.com/rich-iannone/edgecase/blob/main/python/edgecase/graph.py#L49694-L49808){target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"}\n:::\n\n::: {.doc-signature .doc-function}\n```pythonsignature\nGraph.symmetric_difference(other)\n```\n:::\n\n::: {.doc-text}\n\n![Symmetric difference](../assets/graphs/symmetric-difference.light.svg){.lightbox fig-alt=\"G and H share edges A–B and B–D; A–C is only in G (accent) and C–D only in H (teal). The symmetric difference keeps the edges in exactly one graph, A–C and C–D; the shared edges are dashed and node B, left with no edges, is dropped.\" width=\"100%\"}\n\nReturns a new graph containing edges that are in exactly one of the two graphs. Equivalent\nto `(G1 - G2) ∪ (G2 - G1)`.\n\nEdges are matched by their endpoint node IDs (in the same direction, for directed\ngraphs), regardless of weights or attributes. The result is everything that differs\nbetween the two graphs, with the shared part removed, which makes it useful for:\n\n- surveying all changes between two snapshots of a network in a single graph\n- measuring how far apart two networks are (e.g., the number of edges in the result is\n  the edge-edit distance between two graphs on the same node IDs)\n- highlighting disagreement between two inferred or reported networks\n\nNodes are matched by ID, so both graphs must use the same numbering for the same\nentities. Only endpoints of the remaining edges are kept. Each remaining edge keeps the\nweight and attributes from the graph it came from. The result does not record which\ngraph an edge came from; call `difference()` in each direction when you need to\ndistinguish additions from removals. Edges from this graph keep their IDs; edges from\n`other` get fresh IDs above every ID this graph has used. Neither input graph is\nmodified.\n:::\n\n## Parameters {.doc-parameters}\n\n::: {.doc-definition-items}\n<code>[other]{.doc-parameter-name}[:]{.doc-parameter-annotation-sep} [Graph]{.doc-parameter-annotation}</code>\n\n:   The graph to compare with. It must have the same directedness as this graph. Its edges\n    that this graph lacks are included in the result with their own weights and\n    attributes; for nodes, this graph's labels and attributes win and `other` fills in\n    what is missing.\n:::\n\n## Returns {.doc-returns}\n\n::: {.doc-definition-items}\n<code> [Graph]{.doc-parameter-annotation}</code>\n\n:   A new graph containing edges in exactly one of the graphs, plus their endpoints. It is\n    weighted if either input is weighted.\n:::\n\n## Raises {.doc-raises}\n\n::: {.doc-definition-items}\n<code> [ValueError]{.doc-parameter-annotation}</code>\n\n:   If the graphs have different directedness, or if the same label belongs to different\n    node IDs among the nodes kept from the two graphs.\n:::\n\n## Technical Notes {.doc-technical-notes}\n\nSymmetric difference keeps edges present in exactly one of the two graphs, dropping\nany node that ends up edge-isolated. Unlike `difference()` it is symmetric: the\nsame set comes back regardless of which graph makes the call. Both operands must\nagree on directedness. Each surviving edge keeps the weight and attributes it had in\nits own graph; node attributes are merged, with the calling graph winning ties. This\nis the go-to operation for surveying all changes between two network snapshots\nwithout having to reason about direction.\n\n## See Also {.doc-see-also}\n\n[](`~difference`) \n\n:   Keep the edges that the other graph lacks.\n\n[](`~intersection`) \n\n:   Keep the edges common to two graphs.\n\n[](`~union`) \n\n:   Combine two graphs, matching nodes by ID.\n\n## Examples {.doc-examples}\n\nThe edges in one graph or the other but not both are kept. A triangle and a path on the\nsame three nodes differ only in the triangle's closing edge:\n\n::: {#dbecb864 .cell execution_count=1}\n``` {.python .cell-code}\nimport edgecase as ec\n\ntriangle = ec.Graph().add_cycle_graph(3)\npath = ec.Graph().add_path_graph(3)\n\ntriangle.symmetric_difference(path).get_edges_str_repr()\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=1}\n```\n['3--1']\n```\n:::\n:::\n\n\n### Everything that changed\n\n`ec.friends()` is a network of friendships in 2024, and `snapshot=2` gives the same people\na year later, with 8 new friendships. Three of the 2025 friendships ended in 2026. The\nsnapshots share their node IDs, so the symmetric difference of 2024 and 2026 holds every\nchange across the two years, 11 in all, with no record of which way each one went:\n\n::: {#ed63c08f .cell execution_count=2}\n``` {.python .cell-code}\ny2024 = ec.friends()\ny2026 = (\n    ec.friends(snapshot=2)\n    .delete_edge(\"Dev\", \"Femi\")\n    .delete_edge(\"Gwen\", \"Lou\")\n    .delete_edge(\"Rosa\", \"Sven\")\n)\n\nchanges = y2024.symmetric_difference(y2026)\nchanges.count_nodes(), changes.count_edges()\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=2}\n```\n(16, 11)\n```\n:::\n:::\n\n\n`difference()` sorts them into the 8 new ties and the 3 lost ones. Calling the symmetric\ndifference from the other graph gives the same 11:\n\n::: {#1c2e5936 .cell execution_count=3}\n``` {.python .cell-code}\n(\n    y2026.difference(y2024).count_edges(),\n    y2024.difference(y2026).count_edges(),\n    y2026.symmetric_difference(y2024).count_edges(),\n)\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=3}\n```\n(8, 3, 11)\n```\n:::\n:::\n\n\n::: {#9187ceb7 .cell execution_count=4}\n``` {.python .cell-code}\nchanges.plot(color_by=\"club\", label_by=\"label\", seed=1)\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=4}\n```{=html}\n<div class=\"ec-theme\" style=\"display:contents\"><style class=\"ec-theme-vars\">:where(.ec-theme){--ect-3239c383:#fafafa;--ect-e5dfd507:#94a3b8;--ect-8a2de1d4:#374151;--ect-57425a7e:rgba(15,23,42,0.88);--ect-21ade003:#f1f5f9;--ect-61169ea5:#c2410c;--ect-f59ba8df:#a1a1aa}@media (prefers-color-scheme: dark){.ec-theme{--ect-3239c383:#0d1117;--ect-e5dfd507:#4b5563;--ect-8a2de1d4:#d1d5db;--ect-57425a7e:rgba(241,245,249,0.92);--ect-21ade003:#0f172a;--ect-61169ea5:#f97316;--ect-f59ba8df:#71717a}}:is(html.quarto-light, body.quarto-light, html[data-bs-theme=\"light\"], [data-jp-theme-light=\"true\"], body.vscode-light, body.vscode-high-contrast-light, html[theme=\"light\"]) .ec-theme{--ect-3239c383:#fafafa;--ect-e5dfd507:#94a3b8;--ect-8a2de1d4:#374151;--ect-57425a7e:rgba(15,23,42,0.88);--ect-21ade003:#f1f5f9;--ect-61169ea5:#c2410c;--ect-f59ba8df:#a1a1aa}:is(html.quarto-dark, body.quarto-dark, html[data-bs-theme=\"dark\"], [data-jp-theme-light=\"false\"], body.vscode-dark, body.vscode-high-contrast:not(.vscode-high-contrast-light), html[theme=\"dark\"]) .ec-theme{--ect-3239c383:#0d1117;--ect-e5dfd507:#4b5563;--ect-8a2de1d4:#d1d5db;--ect-57425a7e:rgba(241,245,249,0.92);--ect-21ade003:#0f172a;--ect-61169ea5:#f97316;--ect-f59ba8df:#71717a}</style><div class=\"ec-plot\" style=\"position:relative;width:fit-content;max-width:100%;margin:1em auto\">\n<style>.ec-plot > svg { display:block; max-width:100%; height:auto; }</style>\n<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"700\" height=\"500\" viewBox=\"14.0 -19.2 781.9 558.5\" style=\"font-family: -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, 'Segoe UI', Roboto, 'Helvetica Neue', Arial, sans-serif;\">\n<rect x=\"14.0\" y=\"-19.2\" width=\"781.9\" height=\"558.5\" fill=\"var(--ect-3239c383)\"/>\n<line x1=\"225.0\" y1=\"311.8\" x2=\"300.0\" y2=\"356.1\" stroke=\"#4e79a7\" stroke-width=\"2.04\" stroke-opacity=\"0.4\" stroke-linecap=\"round\"/>\n<line x1=\"225.0\" y1=\"311.8\" x2=\"300.0\" y2=\"356.1\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"transparent\" stroke-width=\"8.0\" pointer-events=\"stroke\" data-edge=\"3-5-0\" data-attrs='{\"id\": 48, \"edge\": \"Cleo \\u2014 Ella\", \"since\": 2025}'/>\n<line x1=\"73.9\" y1=\"217.9\" x2=\"87.0\" y2=\"210.4\" stroke=\"#f28e2c\" stroke-width=\"2.04\" stroke-opacity=\"0.4\" stroke-linecap=\"round\"/>\n<line x1=\"73.9\" y1=\"217.9\" x2=\"87.0\" y2=\"210.4\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"transparent\" stroke-width=\"8.0\" pointer-events=\"stroke\" data-edge=\"14-17-1\" data-attrs='{\"id\": 49, \"edge\": \"Nico \\u2014 Rosa\